Checked and unchecked API contract
==================================

TSL follows the zero-overhead C++ convention: the unsuffixed operation performs
the requested operation without hidden input sanitization. Its documented
preconditions are part of the call contract. In Rust, violating a precondition
that can cause undefined behavior requires an ``unsafe`` call; C++ expresses
the same obligation in documentation.

A ``*_checked`` companion exists only when the compiler has a typed plan that
can validate every catastrophic runtime precondition before side effects. The
suffix is deliberately absent when a bare pointer cannot establish provenance,
capacity, overlap, or another required fact. The checked-API census records
those omissions rather than emitting a misleading partial check.

C++ checked values
------------------

A value-returning C++ checked function returns the ordinary value type and
takes a final ``tsl::precondition_error&``. On success it writes ``none``. On
failure it writes the specific error, does not invoke the ordinary operation,
and returns a fully initialized placeholder whose bits have no TSL-defined
meaning. C++ cannot force inspection of the error; callers must inspect it
before using the value. Void checked functions return the error directly.

.. literalinclude:: checked_api_example.cpp
   :language: cpp
   :caption: Both C++ paths; the checked value is consumed only after inspection.

Rust checked values
-------------------

Rust checked functions return ``Result<T, PreconditionError>`` or
``Result<(), PreconditionError>``. The language enforces handling only to the
extent chosen by the caller; a program may still explicitly discard a
``Result``. The representation-neutral root facade retains its normal safe
slice and owned-value conveniences.

Residual obligations
--------------------

Checks validate values expressible in the checked signature. They cannot prove
that a forged C++ span or Rust reference denotes a live object, that a raw
deallocation pointer has matching provenance, or that concurrent mutation
cannot invalidate a view. Constructing and retaining valid language-level
objects remains the caller's responsibility. The documentation of each checked
identity lists its specific errors and any remaining range obligation.

Distribution policy
-------------------

TSL 1.0 is distributed as a compiler-selected generated product. Consumers
should request the primitive and machine-profile slice they deploy. A
monolithic all-profile generation is a stress/reference ceiling, not the
recommended registry or application artifact. Checked wrappers are emitted
once per public callable family and delegate to the selected ordinary
specialization.
